  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 302 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCooler but still near to above normal temperatures are expected this \nweek along with an active stretch through mid week.\n\nA couple of weak upper level waves moving through the area today \nwill continue to promote widespread low cloud cover and occasional \npatchy drizzle owing to weak forcing and shallow low level moisture \nremaining in place across the area. This will limit diurnal \ntemperature range with lows this morning likely no cooler than the \nlow to mid 50s, and highs this afternoon eking out a  modest \nrecovery into the low to mid 60s.\n\nSome breaks in the cloud cover are expected later tonight into \nTuesday, which, along with good low level mixing and strong warm \nadvection ahead of a potent approaching mid week system will push \ntemperatures to their warmest of the forecast period on Tuesday, \nwith highs likely well back into the 70s. This reprieve will be \nbrief, however, with a cold front poised to push steadily through \nthe area late Tuesday night into Wednesday, producing widespread \nshowers and perhaps a few embedded rumbles of thunder, with \nlingering precip likely to taper off late Wednesday. Forecast \nprofiles continue to depict very strong deep layer shear but \nfunctionally zero upright instability, which will largely eliminate \nany sort of organized severe threat, although there is always the \nconcern that a stronger shower could briefly mix down some stronger \ngusts to the surface as a 50-60KT low level jet traverses the area \nwith the boundary. Outside of showers, synoptic winds will be fairly \nstrong ahead of and immediately behind the boundary Tuesday into \nTuesday night, with gusts of 35-40+ MPH possible.\n\nConditions will turn cooler again in the wake of the front, though \nwill still be near seasonal normals, with highs toward the end of \nthe period in the upper 50s to low 60s and lows in the mid 30s to \nmid 40s. A few periods will need at least slight chances for showers \nas models depict the large scale upper low slowing and pivoting over \nthe Great Lakes and eastern CONUS late week into early next week, \nand likely another upper level disturbance pinwheeling around or \nbeing absorbed into the larger scale low, which may bring additional \nshowers and perhaps reinforcing shots of cool air to the area.",
